body:
Bindings take free slots in declaration order. In a scope with many bindings, a heavily used binding declared late gets a fresh slot that no other scope shares, and often ends up with a two-character name. Meanwhile the scope's first slots are reused by inner scopes for their parameters and locals, so they get one-character names no matter how rarely the outer bindings in them are used.

This PR swaps a few such bindings into the front of the scope:
- only in scopes with at least 100 mangle candidates;
- at most 20 swaps per scope, each with the coldest of the first 54 bindings;
- only when the late binding has more than 4× the uses of the one it displaces;
- parameters never move, so `function(e,t,n)` patterns survive.

The promoted binding's uses join a slot that already gets a one-character name, and its old slot cools down, freeing a one-character name for another slot. Everything else keeps declaration order, which is what keeps neighbouring names similar for gzip.

### Example

Input (the full file is in the details below):

```js
export function outer() {
  var c0, c1, /* … */ c99;                  // 100 cold bindings, declared first
  function set(v) { c0 = v; /* … */ c99 = v; }
  function g(p0, /* … */ p53) {              // 54 hot params, reusing the front slots
    return [p0, /* … */ p53, /* ×12 */];
  }
  var hot = g();                             // hot binding declared late
  return [hot, /* ×13 */, set, c0, /* … */ c99];
}
```

No playground link: the input needs 100+ bindings, and its share URL (about 8 KB) is too long to embed here. Paste the full input into playground.oxc.rs with compress and mangle enabled to see the before output; the playground runs the released version, not this branch.

